2. Prediction model and optimization
------------------------------------
State x = [q; v] has 14 components. Input tau_total has 7 components.
Nominal free-space dynamics:
  q_dot = v
  v_dot = M(q)^(-1) * (tau_total - c(q,v) - g(q))
where c(q,v) denotes Coriolis/centrifugal torque, not the C matrix itself.
Use a validated FR3 model, correct joint order, tool inertia and payload.
Evaluate dynamics at predicted states and integrate to obtain x[k+1]=F(x[k],u[k]).
The Python teaching simulation's independent-joint parameters are not FR3 data.

Optimize timed joint tracking over a finite horizon, with position/velocity,
torque and torque-rate constraints and explicit solver status checks.
An initial state outside limits needs defined rejection/recovery behavior.
A nominal model alone does not establish robustness to uncertain payload/contact.

Maintain a documented mapping between modeled total torque and the installed
Franka effort-command convention. Check driver gravity compensation and model
payload consistency; never send gravity twice or assume torque conventions match.

