4. Dependencies for the proposed approach
-----------------------------------------
ROS 2 Jazzy / ament_cmake: build and ROS integration.
controller_interface, hardware_interface, pluginlib: controller plugin.
rclcpp, rclcpp_lifecycle, realtime_tools: lifecycle and data exchange.
trajectory_msgs: timed reference messages.
Eigen3: C++ linear algebra.
Compatible franka_ros2/libfranka: robot driver; franka_semantic_components if used.
Pinocchio + validated FR3 model: predicted nonlinear dynamics.
CasADi with IPOPT: optimization and derivatives; confirm compatible C++ builds
and differentiable model integration rather than assuming numeric Pinocchio
calls automatically become CasADi expressions.
Threads: solver worker.
HPP-FCL is optional for obstacle-distance constraints.
Crocoddyl and qpOASES are alternative solver paths, not mandatory additions.
Vision dependencies are unnecessary for initial joint tracking.

5. Control loop and reference integration
-----------------------------------------
Read the seven position and seven velocity state interfaces in the hardware
update loop. Request exactly the seven fr3_jointN/effort command interfaces.
Run blocking nonlinear solves outside that loop. Transfer state snapshots,
reference IDs/start times and valid timestamped solutions without blocking
the update loop. Account for solver latency when selecting the command.
Implement and test torque/rate limits, stale-result handling, invalid feedback,
solver failure, activation/deactivation and trajectory completion.
Holding the last torque or writing zero is not automatically a safe fallback.

The reference is a timed JointTrajectory, not nav_msgs/Path.
MoveIt may supply the reference without executing it during an NMPC run.
The current reference bridge rejects multi-segment display trajectories; an
approach plus Cartesian plan needs explicit sequence/timing handling.
Reference start timing must agree with actual tracking start.

Keep fr3_arm_controller active during development.
After validation, controller-manager switching must release its effort
interfaces before the new NMPC controller can claim them. Do not run competing
arm command owners. Broadcasters can remain active.
No controller-switch or robot-motion commands are supplied in this document.

6. Validation and experimental comparison
-----------------------------------------
First validate model quantities and predicted dynamics offline.
Then validate closed-loop tracking and fault handling in a compatible effort
simulation. Finally follow laboratory procedures for hardware integration.
Record the same reference, measured states, applied commands, tracking errors,
solver duration, solution age and deadline misses for separate baseline and
NMPC trials, with matching initial state, speed and payload.
Measured hardware improvement cannot be inferred from a synthetic-model plot.

